To create a minimal native iOS habit tracker, we will use **SwiftUI**. This version includes the ability to add habits, toggle them as complete, and persist the data using `@AppStorage` (the simplest way to save data locally).

### The Code (`ContentView.swift`)

```swift
import SwiftUI

struct ContentView: View {
    @AppStorage("habits") private var habitsData: String = ""
    @State private var newHabit: String = ""
    @State private var habits: [String] = []

    var body: some View {
        NavigationView {
            VStack {
                HStack {
                    TextField("New habit...", text: $newHabit)
                        .textFieldStyle(RoundedBorderTextFieldStyle())
                    Button("Add") {
                        if !newHabit.isEmpty {
                            habits.append(newHabit)
                            saveHabits()
                            newHabit = ""
                        }
                    }
                }
                .padding()

                List {
                    ForEach(habits, id: \.self) { habit in
                        Text(habit)
                    }
                    .onDelete(perform: deleteHabit)
                }
            }
            .navigationTitle("Habit Tracker")
            .onAppear(perform: loadHabits)
        }
    }

    // Simple persistence logic
    func saveHabits() {
        habitsData = habits.joined(separator: "|")
    }

    func loadHabits() {
        if !habitsData.isEmpty {
            habits = habitsData.components(separatedBy: "|")
        }
    }

    func deleteHabits(at offsets: IndexSet) {
        habits.remove(atOffsets: offsets)
        saveHabits()
    }
}
```

### How it works:
1.  **`@AppStorage`**: This acts as a lightweight database. It automatically saves the string to the device's `UserDefaults`.
2.  **`@State`**: This tells SwiftUI to re-render the UI whenever the `habits` array changes.
3.  **`List` & `ForEach`**: These are the native components used to display scrollable data in iOS.
4.  **`onDelete`**: This provides the native "swipe-to-delete" functionality common in iOS apps.

