### 2. Real-time Logic (`app.js`)
This script handles the connection to Firebase Firestore. When a user drags a card, the database updates, and all other connected clients see the change instantly.

```javascript
import { initializeApp } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/9.x/firebase-app.js";
import { getFirestore, collection, onSnapshot, doc, updateDoc, addDoc } from "https://www.gstatic.com/firebasejs/9.x/firebase-firestore.js";

const firebaseConfig = { /* Your Firebase Config Object */ };
const app = initializeApp(firebaseConfig);
const db = getFirestore(app);

// Initialize SortableJS
const lists = document.querySelectorAll('.task-list');
lists.forEach(list => {
    new Sortable(list, {
        group: 'shared',
        onEnd: (evt) => {
            const taskId = evt.item.dataset.id;
            const newStatus = evt.to.id.replace('-list', '');
            updateDoc(doc(db, "tasks", taskId), { status: newStatus });
        }
    });
});

// Real-time listener
onSnapshot(collection(db, "tasks"), (snapshot) => {
    snapshot.docChanges().forEach((change) => {
        if (change.type === "added" || change.type === "modified") {
            renderTask(change.doc);
        }
    });
});

function renderTask(doc) {
    const task = doc.data();
    let el = document.querySelector(`[data-id="${doc.id}"]`);
    if (!el) {
        el = document.createElement('div');
        el.className = "bg-white p-3 mb-2 rounded shadow cursor-move";
        el.dataset.id = doc.id;
        document.getElementById(`${task.status}-list`).appendChild(el);
    }
    el.innerText = task.title;
}
```

### 3. Key Technical Considerations

#### A. Real-time Synchronization
By using `onSnapshot` in Firebase, you don't need to write "refresh" logic. When one user drags a task from "To Do" to "Done," the `updateDoc` call triggers a database event that pushes the new state to every other browser tab currently open.

#### B. Drag-and-Drop
We use **SortableJS** because it handles the complex DOM manipulation required for reordering elements. It provides an `onEnd` callback, which we use to sync the new position/status back to the database.

#### C. Scalability Improvements
To make this production-ready, you should add:
1.  **Authentication:** Use Firebase Auth to ensure only team members can move tasks.
2.  **Optimistic UI:** Update the UI immediately when the user drags, and revert if the database request fails.
3.  **Debouncing:** If you have many users, don't update the database on every single pixel movement; update only when the drop is finalized.
4.  **Backend Validation:** Use Firestore Security Rules to prevent users from deleting tasks or moving them to unauthorized columns.

