#### A. The Ring Buffer (Inter-thread Communication)
Using a lock-free queue allows the Gateway thread to pass orders to the Engine thread without context switching.

```cpp
#include <atomic>
#include <vector>

template<typename T, size_t Size>
class RingBuffer {
    T buffer[Size];
    std::atomic<size_t> head{0};
    std::atomic<size_t> tail{0};

public:
    bool push(const T& item) {
        size_t t = tail.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        size_t next_t = (t + 1) % Size;
        if (next_t == head.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // Full
        buffer[t] = item;
        tail.store(next_t, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }

    bool pop(T& item) {
        size_t h = head.load(std::memory_order_relaxed);
        if (h == tail.load(std::memory_order_acquire)) return false; // Empty
        item = buffer[h];
        head.store((h + 1) % Size, std::memory_order_release);
        return true;
    }
};
```

#### B. The Matching Engine (Single-Threaded Core)
To avoid cache misses and lock contention, the matching engine should run on a **pinned CPU core** and handle all logic sequentially.

