### Minimal Implementation (Java)

You will need the [LMAX Disruptor](https://lmax-exchange.github.io/disruptor/) dependency.

```java
import com.lmax.disruptor.RingBuffer;
import com.lmax.disruptor.dsl.Disruptor;
import java.util.concurrent.Executors;

public class HighFreqSystem {
    // Event object pre-allocated to prevent GC
    static class MessageEvent {
        long timestamp;
        public void set(long ts) { this.timestamp = ts; }
    }

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int bufferSize = 1024 * 64; // Must be power of 2
        Disruptor<MessageEvent> disruptor = new Disruptor<>(
            MessageEvent::new, bufferSize, Executors.defaultThreadFactory());

        // Latency monitoring consumer
        disruptor.handleEventsWith((event, sequence, endOfBatch) -> {
            long latency = System.nanoTime() - event.timestamp;
            // In production, use a Histogram (e.g., HdrHistogram) instead of println
            if (sequence % 1_000_000 == 0) System.out.println("Latency: " + latency + "ns");
        });

        RingBuffer<MessageEvent> ringBuffer = disruptor.start();

        // Producer loop
        for (long i = 0; i < 10_000_000; i++) {
            long sequence = ringBuffer.next();
            try {
                MessageEvent event = ringBuffer.get(sequence);
                event.set(System.nanoTime());
            } finally {
                ringBuffer.publish(sequence);
            }
        }
    }
}
```

### Key Principles for "Millions per Second"

1.  **Zero Allocation:** Notice `MessageEvent::new` is called only once during initialization. We reuse the same object in the ring buffer. If you allocate `new Message()` inside the loop, the GC will trigger and latency will spike to milliseconds.
2.  **CPU Affinity:** In a production environment, use [Java-Thread-Affinity](https://github.com/OpenHFT/Java-Thread-Affinity) to pin your producer and consumer threads to specific physical CPU cores. This prevents the OS from moving threads between cores, which invalidates L1/L2 caches.
3.  **Batching:** If throughput drops, increase the batch size. The Disruptor handles this automatically via the `endOfBatch` flag in the consumer.
4.  **Avoid `System.out.println`:** In the example above, I used it for demonstration. In a real system, use a **lock-free circular buffer** to log metrics to a separate thread, or use a library like [Agrona](https://github.com/real-logic/agrona) to write to a memory-mapped file.

