CPU_ZERO(&cpuset);
CPU_SET(target_core_id, &cpuset);
pthread_setaffinity_np(pthread_self(), sizeof(cpu_set_t), &cpuset);
```

#### 3. Busy Polling
Do not use interrupts. Interrupts force the CPU to save state and switch context. Use **Busy Polling** (as shown in the `while(1)` loop), where the CPU constantly checks the NIC ring buffer. This keeps the CPU pipeline hot and ready.

#### 4. Cache Alignment
Ensure your data structures are aligned to the CPU cache line (usually 64 bytes). Use `__rte_cache_aligned` in your structs to prevent "False Sharing," where two cores fight over the same cache line.

#### 5. Bypass the Network Stack
If you need to process the payload (e.g., TCP termination), do not use the Linux `socket()` API. Use a user-space TCP stack like **[f-stack](https://github.com/f-stack/f-stack)** or **[mTCP](https://github.com/eunyoung14/mtcp)**, which runs on top of DPDK.

