### The Approach: Welford’s Algorithm
We use a **Running Standard Deviation** (Welford's method) to track the mean and variance without storing a large buffer of historical data. If a new reading deviates by more than $N$ standard deviations from the mean, it is flagged as an anomaly.

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <math.h>
#include <stdbool.h>

typedef struct {
    float mean;
    float m2;       // Sum of squares of differences from the mean
    int count;
    float threshold; // Number of standard deviations (e.g., 3.0)
} AnomalyDetector;

// Initialize the detector
void init_detector(AnomalyDetector *d, float threshold) {
    d->mean = 0.0f;
    d->m2 = 0.0f;
    d->count = 0;
    d->threshold = threshold;
}

// Process a new sample and return true if it's an anomaly
bool process_sample(AnomalyDetector *d, float sample) {
    d->count++;
    float delta = sample - d->mean;
    d->mean += delta / d->count;
    float delta2 = sample - d->mean;
    d->m2 += delta * delta2;

    if (d->count < 10) return false; // Warm-up period

    float variance = d->m2 / (d->count - 1);
    float std_dev = sqrtf(variance);

    // Check if sample is outside the threshold
    if (fabsf(sample - d->mean) > (d->threshold * std_dev)) {
        return true; // Anomaly detected
    }
    return false;
}

// Example Usage
