### Key Strategies for High Performance

If you are building this for production, you must implement these four optimizations:

#### 1. Edge-Triggered (ET) Mode
In the code above, I used `EPOLLET`. This notifies you only when the state of the socket changes. It is significantly faster than Level-Triggered mode because it reduces the number of system calls, but it requires you to read until the buffer is empty (EAGAIN).

#### 2. Zero-Copy (sendfile)
Don't read file contents into user-space memory if you are just serving files. Use the `sendfile()` system call. It transfers data directly from the disk cache to the network card buffer in the kernel, bypassing the CPU-intensive user-space copy.

#### 3. Thread-per-Core (Affinity)
Don't just use a thread pool. Pin specific threads to specific CPU cores (CPU Affinity). This prevents the OS scheduler from moving threads between cores, which destroys L1/L2 cache locality.

#### 4. Kernel Bypass (Advanced)
For ultra-high performance (e.g., 10Gbps+), consider **DPDK (Data Plane Development Kit)**. It allows your application to take direct control of the Network Interface Card (NIC), bypassing the Linux kernel networking stack entirely.

